module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_test_deployment_acts_on_the_prod_deployments_state

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_test_deployment_acts_on_the_prod_deployments_state: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_test_deployment_acts_on_the_prod_deployments_state" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a deployment that is not the prod-role holder reads, writes, tears down or impersonates state, a singleton or an external attachment that belongs to the deployment that is. Before gunbc.deployment_risk no carrier distinguished the two: which deployment was prod was spelled only in constructor names (srv1_live_dashboard_instance), in literals (gunbc.live_deploy.spec gunbc_approval_broker_owning_instance = \"srv1-live\"), and in host paths every srv1 deployment shared.",

    "HARM: the wrong-instance action lands on the one deployment whose mistakes cost the operator's working day and the shared approval channel, while the deployment that performed it is disposable. The asymmetry is the whole point -- a lab exists so its mistakes cost nothing, and this class is the lab's mistake being charged to prod.",

    "RECEIPTS, four in four different mechanisms, which is what makes it a class and not an incident. (1) A lab twin's retract removed the prod artifact store, because produced artifacts were rostered Owned and Owned means torn down by retract (gunbc.live_deploy.spec, the owned-roster note). (2) serve_function was dropped from the spec and the emitter fell back to a literal, so a lab twin served as prod (gunbc.live_deploy.spec, the serve_function note). (3) A lab apply would have overwritten prod's approval-broker unit -- the reasoning that kept the broker out of a twin's retract covered only half the hazard (review 68094, gunbc.live_deploy.spec). (4) belt_actuate_workdir projected srv1_gunbc_repo_root, so a belt tick ran `git worktree add` into srv1's prod tree (gunbc.roadmap_belt_tick_cli).",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the shared resource is reached by NAME or PATH (a literal, a host-layout row, a constructor) rather than through a derived role; the acting deployment has no class of its own to refuse on; and each repair so far was local to the link where the harm appeared, so the next mechanism reached the same state again (DESIGN 6b).",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable -- each specimen was caught or contained after it was written, by review, a red claim, or an emit-time refusal for that one path.",

    "CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed. Once every external attachment and role-following singleton is reached only through gunbc.deployment_risk resolve_deployment_bindings over prod_role_selection, a TestRisk deployment has no route to a ProdRisk binding that the resolver does not refuse. It cannot be 4: a host path is still a string any author can spell, so the state stays describable in source.",

    "CLIMB STARTED BY THE MODEL PR: the class is derived, the bindings resolve per exact class, and test.claim.deployment_risk_witness_test carries the discriminating RED (a TestRisk deployment refuses rather than resolving the prod topic and app) beside its positive control. That covers the two attachments the binding set carries today and none of the four receipts above, so the row's rung is unchanged by it.",

    "PINS REPOINTED BY DELIVERABLE 2: the role-following singletons follow gunbc.roadmap_dashboard_instance prod_role_realization -- one lookup, the instance roster filtered by its own deployment field -- which REFUSES with a typed ProdRoleRealizationRefusal under NoProdRole, an unrealized holder or an ambiguous one, and mints the only holder token (ProdRoleHolder, sole_constructor). A spec carries that token as gunbc.live_deploy.spec RoleSingletonHolding and every singleton member -- the approval broker unit and front door, the fabric store unit, route, socket, root and directories -- is built from it, never from a caller-spelled instance_id, so they move between targets as one subject. The apply first assesses the desired realization against an observation of every roster host (gunbc.live_deploy.role_singleton_convergence): Noop, Install only when nothing is observed anywhere, and a typed refusal for a move, a removal, an unobservable host, two holders or an ownerless broker. The approval client origin, the GCP IAM converge job's runner (gunbc.fleet_converge_workflow fleet_converge_gcp_iam_converge_job_under, refused under NoProdRole), the approvals app's host (gunbc.auth.approval_broker_placement) and the apply's protected siblings (dashboard_protected_siblings) follow the same realization; broker-side modules receive their host at serve time rather than resolving the role. The REDs are in test.claim.deployment_risk_witness_test at the spec, convergence and emitted-job boundaries.",

    "HONEST NON-PINS, not remaining debt: (a) the nullary serve, belt, dispatch and launch entry points that call srv1_live_dashboard_instance are srv1-live's OWN entry points (HostDashboardInstance.serve_function names roadmap_serve_handle for srv1-live and roadmap_serve_handle_<id> for every other instance, a binding test.claim.serve_function_binding_witness_test now checks roster-wide), so they must NOT follow the role -- following it would make srv1-live act on the new holder's state; (b) gunbc.fleet_reach_endpoint fleet_probe_remote_user_for's srv1/srv2 test is which hosts carry the gunbc-fleet account, a host-shared fact. NOT CLAIMED: that an unbound test deployment refuses everywhere -- roadmap_belt_actuate belt_dispatch_instance_cli still defaults an absent instance env to srv1-live, which stays with the belt owner's lane.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: (1) the role-singleton convergence gains its Move and Remove actuations, so a role change converges the old singleton away instead of refusing; (2) every attachment the srv1 lab shares with prod today (ntfy, Codex account and home, admitted ref, publication identity, R2/GCP/workload identity, OIDC) resolves through DeploymentExternalBindingTable or refuses (deliverable 3). One binding is not sufficient: the class is the route, and any remaining named route is the next receipt.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.deployment_risk", decl_name: "resolve_deployment_bindings", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.deployment_risk_witness_test", decl_name: "a_test_deployment_refuses_rather_than_resolving_the_prod_bindings", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.roadmap_dashboard_instance", decl_name: "prod_role_realization_in", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.deployment_risk_witness_test", decl_name: "moving_the_prod_role_moves_every_role_singleton_member_to_the_new_holders_spec", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.live_deploy.role_singleton_convergence", decl_name: "role_singleton_convergence", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
